WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:17.300
Announcer: From the nation's capital, the
American Enterprise Institute for Public Policy

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:22.820
Research presents Public Policy Forums, a
series of programs featuring the nation's

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:28.060
top authorities presenting their differing
views on the vital issues which confront us.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:32.369
Today's topic, Weapons Against Inflation.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:37.090
Unless you're a visitor just in from outer
space or perhaps you've been living on a desert

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:42.339
island somewhere, you've noticed that the
cost of living has gone up tremendously over

00:00:42.339 --> 00:00:47.860
the last several months to the point where
today it costs twice as much to buy goods

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:53.020
and services as it did to buy the same things
just 12 years ago.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:58.190
President Carter's voluntary anti-inflation
program is aimed at cooling the inflationary

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:00.079
fires, but will it work?

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:02.379
What are the alternatives?

00:01:02.379 --> 00:01:07.150
How great is the possibility of a recession
sometime during 1979?

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:10.030
If it comes, how serious will it be?

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:16.030
Is the President likely to ask for and perhaps
receive permission from Congress perhaps even

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:22.530
stand by permission to invoke mandatory wage
and price controls in the near future?

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:28.140
And what was our experience the last time
the country had mandatory economic controls?

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.520
Some of the country's biggest companies have
promised to go along with the Carter anti-inflation

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.750
program.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:39.140
But what about labor, especially with major
contract negotiations in the works during

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.000
1979?

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:46.800
Welcome to another Public Policy Forum presented
by AEI, the American Enterprise Institute,

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.690
a nonprofit, nonpartisan research and education
organization.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:56.790
This roundtable discussion will explore the
topic, Weapons Against Inflation.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:01.810
Appearing on our panel today are Alfred E.
Kahn, Chairman of the Council on Wage and

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:02.970
Price Stability.

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:09.030
Dr. Kahn, with a PhD in economics from Yale
University, is counselor to the President

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:13.340
for inflation, Mr. Carter's chief inflation
fighter.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:18.200
Before taking over as the man in charge of
the voluntary wage and price control program,

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:24.090
Dr. Kahn headed the Civil Aeronautics Board,
where he helped bring about airline deregulation.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:30.560
Alan Greenspan, former chairman of the President's
Council of Economic Advisers under President

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.060
Gerald Ford.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:36.690
Dr. Greenspan's PhD in economics is from New
York University.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:42.470
He is presently president of an economics
consulting firm, Townsend Greenspan, in New

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:43.490
York City.

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:49.940
Dr. Greenspan has held numerous government
positions, including the chairmanship of four

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:52.120
White House economic committees.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:59.190
Rudolph A. Oswald is director of research
for the National Labor Organization, the AFL-CIO.

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:06.750
He's a former Fulbright scholar who holds
a PhD also in economics from Georgetown University.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:12.780
Dr. Oswald has devoted much of his professional
career researching the effect of the nation's

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:17.170
economy on the well-being of American workers.

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:23.650
Marvin Kosters is a resident scholar at AEI
and director of its Center for the Study of

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:25.400
Government Regulation.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.370
He too holds a PhD in economics from the University
of Chicago.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:36.390
Dr. Kosters has served as senior staff economist
to the President's Council of Economic Advisers

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:42.690
and as Deputy Assistant to the President for
Economic Affairs during the Ford administration.

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:47.830
He spent three years as Associate Director
of the President's Cost of Living Council.

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:52.650
John Charles Daly will moderate today's roundtable
discussion.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:58.209
Mr. Daly is a former news correspondent and
commentator for CBS News and ABC News.

00:03:58.209 --> 00:04:04.660
He is a former ABC network Vice President
and once headed the Voice of America.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:06.690
Now, here is Mr. Daly.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:11.880
John: This Public Policy Forum, part of a
series presented by the American Enterprise

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:19.470
Institute is concerned with what may well
be called the enigma of free society's inflation.

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:24.530
For nearly a decade and a half, we have been
struggling with an inflationary economy at

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:31.150
home while keeping a wary eye on the often
dramatic struggles with inflation by our colleagues

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:34.540
in the industrial and developing societies
abroad.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:40.900
There's the sense that as with the common
cold, we're not sure where inflation came

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:45.020
from or what we should or can do about it.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:50.740
At the beginning of 1978, the administration
initiated a voluntary program under which

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:57.530
government, business, and labor would cooperate
in an anti-inflation program inspired by persuasion

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.100
and publicity.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:02.050
The results have been less than inspiring.

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:07.620
At the end of 1978, the administration issued
an urgent call to battle.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:14.210
The President outlined a new plan of attack
which in his words will cut the budget deficit,

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:20.150
slash federal hiring, and reduce the federal
workforce, restraint federal pay, delay further

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:28.270
tax cuts, remove needless regulations, use
federal policy to encourage more competition,

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:33.340
set specific standards for both wages and
prices throughout the economy generally limiting

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:38.550
wage increases to 7% and price increases to
five and three quarters percent.

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:43.520
And the President concluded, "We will use
all the powers at our disposal to make this

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:49.370
program work," announcing that legislative
proposals to implement elements of the program

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:50.430
would go to the Congress.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:57.980
In January, the President highlighted his
surprise weapon, a real wage insurance proposal

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:01.550
as an additional incentive for labor cooperation.

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:07.120
Workers who observed the wage standards in
the program would be eligible for a tax rebate

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:10.770
if the inflation rate is more than 7%.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:17.080
These then as of now and if the Congress approves
new legislation are the principal weapons

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:20.229
in our arsenal against inflation.

00:06:20.229 --> 00:06:27.199
Dr. Kahn, the President said in a press conference
recently, "Obviously, with more than 1,000

00:06:27.199 --> 00:06:31.770
different kinds of decisions to be made, there
will be some flexibility."

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:37.389
Do you and the president anticipate any major
modifications now that you've heard from many

00:06:37.389 --> 00:06:42.670
constituencies during the legally mandated
public comment period just concluded?

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:47.930
Dr. Kahn: I don't think one could characterize
the kinds of modifications that we're thinking

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:49.460
about as major.

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:56.290
But there are two kinds that we are very intensively
considering.

00:06:56.290 --> 00:07:04.169
The first is in response to an almost unanimous
criticism by business as well as labor that

00:07:04.169 --> 00:07:09.340
our treatment of fringe benefits and in particular
the catch-up costs of maintaining existing

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:13.690
levels of benefits is simply too stringent.

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:20.780
And therefore, we are contemplating a number
of possible ways of introducing a modicum

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:23.840
of flexibility and treating them.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:28.590
The second is in response to the obvious point
that's been made by labor, but it was made

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:37.000
by me in testimony back in 1971 that a standard
that permits the pass on of uncontrollable

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:43.870
costs with a fixed percentage margin permits
accumulation of profits on the basis of additional

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:50.900
costs, and we're trying to devise ways of
preventing the earning of additional profits

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:52.419
on additional costs.

00:07:52.419 --> 00:07:53.970
John: All right.

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:59.130
Dr. Greenspan, what are the similarities and
differences between President Carter's new

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:02.110
program and President Ford's win program?

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:15.570
Dr. Greenspan: Well, if I can expand President
Ford's win program into two parts, one, a

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:22.830
call for severe restriction on fiscal policy,
federal spending, and on the budget deficit,

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:26.690
and the other, something of a vague freeze.

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:34.020
I would say there is quite a good deal similar
with the same problems.

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:41.019
In my view, all that is desirable and, in
fact, all that is necessary to restrain inflation

00:08:41.019 --> 00:08:47.899
is in the first part of both of those programs
that is the restraint on federal government

00:08:47.899 --> 00:08:54.089
expenditure and indirectly on its credit creating
capacities.

00:08:54.089 --> 00:08:58.480
The second part, namely, the guidelines, I
think are counterproductive.

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:04.819
Actually are not part of an anti-inflation
program, but in my view, actually, ultimately

00:09:04.819 --> 00:09:07.619
add to inflation.

00:09:07.619 --> 00:09:14.990
So I would suspect that the guideline part
was wholly inappropriate response to inflation

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:21.940
under the name win and I would suspect that
would come out pretty much the same with that

00:09:21.940 --> 00:09:24.510
part of President Carter's program.

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:25.790
They're both losers.

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:27.439
John: All right.

00:09:27.439 --> 00:09:30.830
Dr. Oswald, will you...we've already had reference
to it.

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:35.740
Will you summarize organized labor's objections
toward the administration program?

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:41.990
Dr. Oswald: Organized labor's objections are
that it's described as a voluntary program,

00:09:41.990 --> 00:09:46.791
but it really is mandatory in terms of wages,
because there is an enforcement mechanism

00:09:46.791 --> 00:09:53.559
there, which is every employer is willing
to enforce a set guideline of 7%.

00:09:53.559 --> 00:09:58.380
Contrary to what you said in your introduction,
there is not a five and three quarter percent

00:09:58.380 --> 00:09:59.629
guideline on prices.

00:09:59.629 --> 00:10:07.550
As a matter of fact, the only mention of a
price guideline is nine and a half percent.

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:17.459
And it talks about a half of percent deceleration
from sort of price increases that took place

00:10:17.459 --> 00:10:19.009
in 1976, 1977.

00:10:19.009 --> 00:10:26.519
And that says those who raise prices the most
can do so again, and those who are most responsible

00:10:26.519 --> 00:10:29.110
at that time can do less now.

00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:35.610
That hardly seems like an equitable program
because it means that there is not really

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:42.149
a mechanism for controlling prices because
there is no manpower to do it and the consumer

00:10:42.149 --> 00:10:48.519
doesn't know what an appropriate price increase
is and no other source of income is controlled

00:10:48.519 --> 00:10:49.730
except wages.

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:51.470
That's the basic objection.

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:56.320
And therefore, the labor movement said, "If
there are to be controls, let's make them

00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:03.660
by legislation, effectively enforce against
everybody so that they apply effectively against

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:08.850
all segments of the economy, not just against
workers who are working for wages."

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:09.850
John: All right.

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:14.240
Dr. Kosters, you have served with the Council
of Economic Advisors as Associate Director

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:19.459
of the Cost of Living Council and authored
Controls and Inflation, the economic stabilization

00:11:19.459 --> 00:11:24.230
program in retrospect, so you have been in
this battlefield for a long time.

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:27.490
Do you think the President's voluntary program
would work?

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:33.089
Dr. Kosters: Well, I think that there are
two ways of addressing that question.

00:11:33.089 --> 00:11:37.480
The first way would be to ask, is it likely
to break down?

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:43.160
And by breaking down, I mean, will there happen
to be a wage increase or possibly even a price

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:49.129
increase somewhere that seems so much out
of line, what are set out as the standards

00:11:49.129 --> 00:11:51.439
that the whole program gets discredited?

00:11:51.439 --> 00:11:54.319
Now, that's the tendency under any program.

00:11:54.319 --> 00:12:00.670
The program that I was involved in, we said
those were the last cows in the barn.

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:04.489
But one can do that only about so long and
it wears a little bit thin.

00:12:04.489 --> 00:12:07.499
So that's always a risk and there are good
reasons for it.

00:12:07.499 --> 00:12:12.339
The second way to address the question is
to ask, is the rate of inflation likely to

00:12:12.339 --> 00:12:16.329
be lower after it's all over with than it
was before?

00:12:16.329 --> 00:12:21.380
Well, by virtue of the standards themselves,
I would say that's quite unlikely.

00:12:21.380 --> 00:12:26.319
We have a great deal of experience with that
both in Europe and in the United States.

00:12:26.319 --> 00:12:31.839
All of that experience that I'm aware of is
negative and I see no reason why we're likely

00:12:31.839 --> 00:12:33.500
to fare any better under this one.

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:34.500
John: All right.

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:38.079
But now let's come to grip with the weapons
against inflation.

00:12:38.079 --> 00:12:44.089
Dr. Herbert Stein whom you all know has had
a great deal of experience with inflation

00:12:44.089 --> 00:12:49.790
and its problems, has written of the last
decade of our struggles with inflation.

00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:54.529
"Two lessons of this recent history are relevant
today.

00:12:54.529 --> 00:13:01.850
One, do not think we can flirt with controls
and not get them, and two, do not think that

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:07.339
the ineffectiveness of controls which has
roots deep in the American economic and political

00:13:07.339 --> 00:13:12.269
system can be overcome by sufficiently enthusiastic
operators."

00:13:12.269 --> 00:13:13.709
[inaudible 00:13:12].

00:13:13.709 --> 00:13:15.639
Would you respond, Dr. Kahn?

00:13:15.639 --> 00:13:18.790
Dr. Kahn: Well, it's a little difficult to
know where to begin.

00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:33.149
The notion that any kind of controls or incomes
policy can only do harm, it seems to me can

00:13:33.149 --> 00:13:39.589
only spring from an assumption that the level
at which let's say the Teamsters sign for

00:13:39.589 --> 00:13:48.860
wage increase is somehow God-given or competitive
market determined in the same way as let's

00:13:48.860 --> 00:13:52.559
say the free price of natural gas if it were
uncontrolled.

00:13:52.559 --> 00:13:56.459
I submit that there's an enormous difference
between those two.

00:13:56.459 --> 00:14:01.160
If you hold the price of natural gas below
its marginal cost, you're gonna get shortages.

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:04.910
Is there anybody on this panel who believes
that if you can induce the Teamsters by one

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:11.059
device or another to reduce their wage settlement
from 11% to 10% to 9% to 8% to 7%, that you're

00:14:11.059 --> 00:14:16.199
gonna have a shortage of Teamsters that this
is a competitive market, which by University

00:14:16.199 --> 00:14:20.040
of Chicago-God-given standards should not
be interfered with.

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:23.910
I submit that's an absurdity.

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:31.139
Now, I'm not suggesting that these weapons
are total.

00:14:31.139 --> 00:14:34.420
And I'm not, as you know, an enthusiastic
supporter of the guidelines.

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:39.480
I have been quoted correctly as referring
to them as those stinking wage price guidelines.

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:40.720
I don't love them.

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:47.470
But on the other hand, I do not believe it
is logical to speak of a lot of these prices

00:14:47.470 --> 00:14:52.999
and wages as though they are determined in
a competitive market so in such a way that

00:14:52.999 --> 00:14:56.449
to interfere will produce enormous distortions
in the economy.

00:14:56.449 --> 00:14:57.759
I won't go on a great length.

00:14:57.759 --> 00:15:02.869
There's a lot of differences from the win
program which alone attracted me to this.

00:15:02.869 --> 00:15:06.792
One difference is that we don't have any ties
yet which have win on them.

00:15:06.792 --> 00:15:11.589
John: I merely suggest you wait.

00:15:11.589 --> 00:15:13.029
Dr. Kahn: Exactly.

00:15:13.029 --> 00:15:19.509
The notion that all the European experience
is negative is, I submit, a piece of folklore.

00:15:19.509 --> 00:15:24.389
The British experience does suggest that it
is possible by having some sort of incomes

00:15:24.389 --> 00:15:28.889
policy if you have a sufficient acceptance
of the notion that we are in a process of

00:15:28.889 --> 00:15:34.529
mutual frustration, if that becomes sufficiently
widespread that you can, in fact, at least

00:15:34.529 --> 00:15:36.939
for periods of time, induce certain restraints.

00:15:36.939 --> 00:15:42.860
It is not an accident that every Western democratic
capitalist country has attempted in one way

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:46.000
or the other the growth for incomes policy.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:49.699
Conclusion, I'm not at all suggesting that
this is the part of the program that turns

00:15:49.699 --> 00:15:50.899
me on.

00:15:50.899 --> 00:15:55.550
I'm not suggesting that it will do any good
in the absence of monetary and fiscal restraints.

00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:59.769
And I wish you would all mention the fact
that we have a very comprehensive intention

00:15:59.769 --> 00:16:04.339
at least to attack structural defects in the
economy and government interventions that

00:16:04.339 --> 00:16:06.160
inflate costs and prevent competition.

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:11.180
Dr. Oswald: But I think what you've really
described as the controls program, which you

00:16:11.180 --> 00:16:18.929
have attempted to put into effect on the wage
side, without any legal basis for putting

00:16:18.929 --> 00:16:26.910
it into effect, which in a sense denies the
appropriate procedure by which issues can

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:28.660
be handled.

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:36.439
There should be an appropriate body which
has sufficient manpower to hear cases.

00:16:36.439 --> 00:16:43.059
There should be a legal basis for punishing
those that don't adhere rather than by some

00:16:43.059 --> 00:16:50.459
vague threats, which don't really have a legal
basis where you attempt to use laws not for

00:16:50.459 --> 00:16:53.740
the purpose for which they were designed but
for other purposes.

00:16:53.740 --> 00:17:00.850
And if the inflation is really as bad as it
is, if we're going to have these sorts of

00:17:00.850 --> 00:17:02.720
things, then let's do it right.

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:04.280
Let's do it by law.

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:05.530
Let's do it equitably.

00:17:05.530 --> 00:17:10.309
John: Dr. Oswald, would you elucidate what
you mean by using laws in way other than they

00:17:10.309 --> 00:17:11.590
are intended to be used?

00:17:11.590 --> 00:17:16.690
Dr. Oswald: Well, the President indicated
that in order to enforce these guidelines,

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:21.180
I believe that's the words that he used, he
was going to use government procurement actions.

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:25.390
He was going to use the actions of the Export-Import
Bank.

00:17:25.390 --> 00:17:31.480
And since then, he's made allusions to other
laws as a basis for trying to make sure that

00:17:31.480 --> 00:17:33.170
there is compliance.

00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:40.640
Those laws all were established with specific
goals in terms of providing for government

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:47.940
procurement, for providing...for aiding American
exports abroad, a number of other things.

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:52.940
If we're going to have a program that is geared
towards inflation, why not set up a law that

00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:56.810
deals with that directly rather than through
this indirect fashion?

00:17:56.810 --> 00:17:59.100
John: Dr. Kahn, would you like to respond?

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:00.360
Dr. Kahn: Well, I'll be very brief.

00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:06.640
When a lawyer tells me that I'm not following
due process, I tend to hold on to my wallet.

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:10.690
When an economist tells me the same thing,
I'm gonna hold on to my checkbook as well.

00:18:10.690 --> 00:18:13.101
I'm not an expert on the law.

00:18:13.101 --> 00:18:17.900
Our lawyers tell us that it is perfectly reasonable
for the government to use its regulatory authority

00:18:17.900 --> 00:18:23.030
to refuse, for example, to pass on inordinate
wage or cost increases.

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:27.990
And I'm not a lawyer so I guess take it up
with my priest.

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:30.910
John: Dr. Greenspan:

00:18:30.910 --> 00:18:35.790
Dr. Kahn: Chaplain was the word I was groping
for.

00:18:35.790 --> 00:18:40.210
Dr. Greenspan: I presumably you were not referring
to me in that.

00:18:40.210 --> 00:18:45.510
Dr. Kahn: No, I'd never make that mistake.

00:18:45.510 --> 00:18:52.990
Dr. Greenspan: I'd like to go back to some
very important and really fundamental questions,

00:18:52.990 --> 00:18:55.360
which Dr. Kahn raised right in the beginning.

00:18:55.360 --> 00:19:01.700
I think that we will get back to Dr. Oswald's
issue because that's gonna proliferate this

00:19:01.700 --> 00:19:03.220
discussion extensively.

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:12.760
I agree, the Teamsters do not strike me as
a union characterized by extreme competition.

00:19:12.760 --> 00:19:13.830
I would not therefore...

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:17.140
Dr. Oswald: I wish I could be that diplomatic.

00:19:17.140 --> 00:19:22.190
Dr. Greenspan: I would not therefore conclude
that the solution to the problem is guidelines.

00:19:22.190 --> 00:19:30.610
I think that there are obviously far more
effective and long run solutions to that problem

00:19:30.610 --> 00:19:36.290
such as deregulating the truckers which working
through the market mechanism, I think do have

00:19:36.290 --> 00:19:42.290
surprising impact on the Teamsters themselves.

00:19:42.290 --> 00:19:49.810
The question as to whether or not the European
experience is valid or not is extremely difficult

00:19:49.810 --> 00:19:51.140
one.

00:19:51.140 --> 00:19:56.900
I tend to conclude pretty much the way Dr.
Kosters comes out.

00:19:56.900 --> 00:20:01.920
But I will grant you that it is extraordinarily
difficult to read some of that evidence.

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:08.720
I would say the hypothesis that stipulates
that they work is extremely difficult to demonstrate,

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:13.630
and I personally believe impossible, although
I will grant you the opposite is also rather

00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:15.410
difficult.

00:20:15.410 --> 00:20:22.260
The reason I believe that democratic societies
grope for this type of mechanism when confronted

00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:31.110
with inflation is that it perceives this sort
of approach as creating some form of short

00:20:31.110 --> 00:20:32.320
term political benefit.

00:20:32.320 --> 00:20:34.540
And in certain instances, it does.

00:20:34.540 --> 00:20:41.720
I don't deny, for example, that President
Nixon's freeze in August of 1971 did, in fact,

00:20:41.720 --> 00:20:43.540
stop the rate of inflation.

00:20:43.540 --> 00:20:51.070
But the problem that we have is that while
there was this pressure for short term benefits,

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:59.470
there was a growing unawareness of the longer
term costs that such policies invariably generate.

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:06.610
And one of our difficulties that we had in
a societies like ours is that politicians,

00:21:06.610 --> 00:21:13.660
the political system as a whole, tends to
over-emphasize short term benefits in virtually

00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:18.780
all of its actions without fully comprehending
the long term costs.

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:26.060
And I believe that one of the major problems
that guidelines, controls, mandatory, or non-mandatory

00:21:26.060 --> 00:21:31.950
create is they give the illusion of success.

00:21:31.950 --> 00:21:39.560
And they do, in fact, create problems far
greater than I think we conceivably imagine.

00:21:39.560 --> 00:21:43.330
We've had many problems in this area.

00:21:43.330 --> 00:21:50.230
One of the more recent examples is the difficulties
that the legacy of the Nixon control program

00:21:50.230 --> 00:21:57.810
has created for us, namely, the problem in
oil and energy price controls and the difficulties

00:21:57.810 --> 00:22:03.410
that have emerged in unleaded gasoline and
in a number of other areas.

00:22:03.410 --> 00:22:12.770
I submit that what we tend to create are huge
bureaucratic systems which essentially shuffle

00:22:12.770 --> 00:22:20.610
paper and cannot very easily be demonstrated
to have significant impacts on the overall

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:22.650
price or wage level.

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:29.190
Overall, I would tend to argue that in the
longer run, because they thwart productivity,

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:36.200
because they generate imbalances in the system,
they tend over the long run to be pro-inflation

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:37.200
rather than anti-inflation.

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:39.900
John: Dr. Kosters, you wanna taka a shot too?

00:22:39.900 --> 00:22:40.900
Dr. Kosters: Yeah.

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:45.540
Well, I think it's important to recognize
that whenever you have a set of standards,

00:22:45.540 --> 00:22:51.530
whether voluntary or mandatory, the one piece
of policy in the context of all of the policies

00:22:51.530 --> 00:22:52.530
that are going on.

00:22:52.530 --> 00:22:57.060
This, of course, is what makes it difficult
to assess the impact of any given program

00:22:57.060 --> 00:22:58.060
at any time.

00:22:58.060 --> 00:23:00.860
Take, for example, the British case.

00:23:00.860 --> 00:23:05.390
It is true that inflation came down while
the policy was enforced.

00:23:05.390 --> 00:23:09.190
But it's also true that there was a good deal
of belt-tightening going on when the policy

00:23:09.190 --> 00:23:10.190
was implemented.

00:23:10.190 --> 00:23:17.670
And I think that the fact that other policies
are being made at the same time and that they're

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:23.050
being made in the context of the fact that
there is a program of standards creates one

00:23:23.050 --> 00:23:27.340
of the main dangers of such a program.

00:23:27.340 --> 00:23:35.500
Back when the 1971-72 and so on controls were
enforced, it was often said that the controls

00:23:35.500 --> 00:23:40.170
helped to make it possible to have a more
vigorous expansion than we could otherwise

00:23:40.170 --> 00:23:41.250
have.

00:23:41.250 --> 00:23:47.410
At the present time, it's often said that
we need to make a choice between voluntary

00:23:47.410 --> 00:23:52.020
controls, if they are not successful, perhaps
between mandatory controls and recession.

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:58.920
And it seems to me that when one is talking
about that kind of choice, one is talking

00:23:58.920 --> 00:24:05.580
about perhaps stimulating the economy somewhat
more than would otherwise be considered prudent.

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:09.950
And that seems like a formula for more inflation
rather than less inflation from everything

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:10.950
we know.

00:24:10.950 --> 00:24:13.540
Dr. Kahn: Could I just respond very briefly
to that?

00:24:13.540 --> 00:24:18.770
I think this is very useful at narrowing the
controversy between us in some cases to a

00:24:18.770 --> 00:24:22.510
point where there is no controversy.

00:24:22.510 --> 00:24:29.150
There's no question that when the mandatory
controls were imposed in August of 1971 in

00:24:29.150 --> 00:24:34.110
anticipation of an election 15 months ahead
and of course, I'm not impugning the motives

00:24:34.110 --> 00:24:36.780
of the Republicans any more than the Democrats
that would have done the same thing if they

00:24:36.780 --> 00:24:37.780
were in power.

00:24:37.780 --> 00:24:40.300
John: Nor suggesting that history will repeat
itself.

00:24:40.300 --> 00:24:43.690
Dr. Kahn: Precisely.

00:24:43.690 --> 00:24:44.820
Precisely.

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:48.600
You've taken the words exactly out of my mouth.

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:53.710
The imposition of controls at that time was
obviously part of a deliberate attempt to

00:24:53.710 --> 00:24:57.410
inflate the economy and for obvious reasons.

00:24:57.410 --> 00:25:01.500
We were making unsatisfactory progress in
reducing unemployment.

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:04.380
And it was thought that if you could impose
the controls, then you could have massive

00:25:04.380 --> 00:25:08.710
monetary expansion and budgetary looseness.

00:25:08.710 --> 00:25:13.000
And the one was an attempt to make it possible
to do the other.

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:19.170
But we are talking now about a context in
which limitations are being imposed.

00:25:19.170 --> 00:25:25.210
Macro-restraint is being followed, and it
is simply an unwillingness on the part of

00:25:25.210 --> 00:25:30.580
the government and I think the country to
rely exclusively on a jamming out of the monetary

00:25:30.580 --> 00:25:32.310
and fiscal breaks.

00:25:32.310 --> 00:25:38.260
The distortions, it seems to me, are far less
likely to be serious than they were in the

00:25:38.260 --> 00:25:43.850
context of '71 to '73, though I do not look
forward to a permanent continuation of controls

00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:46.450
with any more enthusiasm than you do Mr. Greenspan.

00:25:46.450 --> 00:25:47.450
John: Doctor.

00:25:47.450 --> 00:25:51.610
Dr. Greenspan: Whether or not we turn out
to be in difficulty a year from now will depend

00:25:51.610 --> 00:25:56.290
largely on what happens to monetary and fiscal
policy in the meantime just as it did then.

00:25:56.290 --> 00:26:01.230
Man: Well, I think it's important to look
at the change and the situation.

00:26:01.230 --> 00:26:04.720
At that time, we had an inflation rate of
a little bit over 4%.

00:26:04.720 --> 00:26:08.760
Now, the inflation rate is over nine and a
half percent.

00:26:08.760 --> 00:26:12.170
At that time, food prices were relatively
stable.

00:26:12.170 --> 00:26:17.780
Food prices now are going up at a twelve and
a half percent rate and they are a major factor

00:26:17.780 --> 00:26:21.410
in what consumers buy.

00:26:21.410 --> 00:26:27.850
The interest rates have been going up substantially,
which has been pushing up the housing sectors

00:26:27.850 --> 00:26:30.660
substantially and the cost of housing.

00:26:30.660 --> 00:26:34.600
Medical care then was going up rapidly, is
still going up rapidly.

00:26:34.600 --> 00:26:42.180
And energy costs at that time were not yet
shot off by the OPEC situation and are going

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:49.640
up rapidly now and are expected to go up rapidly
in the next few years unless there is some

00:26:49.640 --> 00:26:51.860
sort of an intervention.

00:26:51.860 --> 00:26:58.191
And monetary and fiscal policy will not address
the problems of food price increases, will

00:26:58.191 --> 00:27:02.630
not address directly the sectorial problems
that exist.

00:27:02.630 --> 00:27:09.470
And I think to just say that there is some
sort of cosmetic here in terms of wage price

00:27:09.470 --> 00:27:15.670
guidelines plus the strong monetary and fiscal
program is going to take care of our inflation

00:27:15.670 --> 00:27:21.220
really doesn't look at some of the pressures
in different segments of the economy which

00:27:21.220 --> 00:27:24.060
are pushing up the price level much more rapidly.

00:27:24.060 --> 00:27:31.450
Man: You know, what basically concerns me
is that his program will beget yours.

00:27:31.450 --> 00:27:37.640
And the reason I say that is that I think
it's unrealistic to presume that you can have

00:27:37.640 --> 00:27:42.990
a stable equilibrium type guidelines program.

00:27:42.990 --> 00:27:45.090
This is not a stable program.

00:27:45.090 --> 00:27:52.740
And if it increases, what will start to occur
is we'll begin to find that any even skillfully

00:27:52.740 --> 00:28:00.030
crafted program of guidelines or standards
must have necessity, be comprehensible in

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:08.320
a political sense, which means it can scarcely
pick up more than at best 95% of all of the

00:28:08.320 --> 00:28:13.770
possible types of wage and price decisions
that wouldn't be made, which is terrific,

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:17.870
except that there are 100 million of them
out there, which means you're dealing with

00:28:17.870 --> 00:28:23.240
5 million decisions as an example, which go
outside the guidelines.

00:28:23.240 --> 00:28:31.270
As you start to try to bring them in, you
begin to get an extraordinarily burgeoning

00:28:31.270 --> 00:28:36.330
system, which ultimately, I fear, is going
to be sanctioned by the Congress as a mandatory

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:42.020
system because it will have all of the characteristics
except the authorizing legislation.

00:28:42.020 --> 00:28:47.420
Man: On the question of the rises in energy
prices and food prices, I would agree with

00:28:47.420 --> 00:28:57.310
this side of the table, which is, well, from
your point of view on the right, totally,

00:28:57.310 --> 00:29:01.880
that the solution for rising energy prices
is not mandatory price control.

00:29:01.880 --> 00:29:06.650
The solution for the rising price of beef
is not mandatory price controls or any kind

00:29:06.650 --> 00:29:12.090
of price controls, and I think therefore,
to the extent that you really are getting

00:29:12.090 --> 00:29:17.880
into a situation in which you're not influencing
what is a real margin of administrative discretion.

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:23.130
But you really are trying to set price below
some sort of equilibrium point.

00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:27.880
You are simply concealing from consumers what
the real marginal cost of those supplies is

00:29:27.880 --> 00:29:29.020
to the economy.

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:32.559
And I do think that way lies disaster.

00:29:32.559 --> 00:29:38.500
The part of the program, however, that I find
terribly attractive is, and again, I recognize

00:29:38.500 --> 00:29:43.720
the difference between promise and performance
that we are attempting systematically to look

00:29:43.720 --> 00:29:48.510
at these particular sectors, which have been
responsible for the preponderance portion

00:29:48.510 --> 00:29:56.880
of the increase in the cost of living and
to try to identify the restrictions on competition,

00:29:56.880 --> 00:30:01.950
the insanities of methods of organizations,
such as the insane method that we have for

00:30:01.950 --> 00:30:06.650
paying for medical care, the interferences
with the free markets, such as we have, as

00:30:06.650 --> 00:30:11.570
part of our agricultural policy or our regulation
on trucking, which, until recently, required

00:30:11.570 --> 00:30:14.650
that you take your truck into a food store
full and take them back empty.

00:30:14.650 --> 00:30:20.230
I mean, in each of these areas, if you look
at building codes and land use restrictions

00:30:20.230 --> 00:30:23.960
and inadequate taxes on land that's held out
of use, there are millions of things that

00:30:23.960 --> 00:30:25.800
can be done in these areas.

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:30.380
But the thing that I think should not...and
I would suggest that the people on my left

00:30:30.380 --> 00:30:34.810
would probably approve of those attacks but
I suggest that the attempt to substitute total

00:30:34.810 --> 00:30:39.360
controls to hold down the price of oil when
the incremental cost of the US economy is

00:30:39.360 --> 00:30:41.600
$14 a barrel is in the long run just a disaster.

00:30:41.600 --> 00:30:47.731
Man: It seems to me that you could probably
make a case for saying that with a 7% wage

00:30:47.731 --> 00:30:53.309
guideline, there'll be a lot of business firms
that do have an incentive to try to enforce

00:30:53.309 --> 00:30:54.309
that.

00:30:54.309 --> 00:31:00.950
On the other hand, with a voluntary price
guideline, like there's, which is a big price

00:31:00.950 --> 00:31:06.840
index, it's an enormously complicated thing
to audit or even to compute.

00:31:06.840 --> 00:31:12.530
It'd be difficult to determine just how much
compliance there is.

00:31:12.530 --> 00:31:18.630
So in that context, it seems to me that if
one feared the possibility that the wage guidelines

00:31:18.630 --> 00:31:25.390
will bite more than the price guidelines that
you would very much favor a reduction in the

00:31:25.390 --> 00:31:32.240
increases in the demand that we've been having
in order to avoid the prospect that wages

00:31:32.240 --> 00:31:37.720
will tend to be held down and prices will
tend to float upward with a fairly buoyant

00:31:37.720 --> 00:31:38.720
demand.

00:31:38.720 --> 00:31:39.720
John: Doctor.

00:31:39.720 --> 00:31:45.110
Man: But reduction and aggregate demand is
not an assurance that we'll have reductions

00:31:45.110 --> 00:31:48.870
in prices.

00:31:48.870 --> 00:31:53.250
Our concern is that what we really will have
is a compounding of the problem.

00:31:53.250 --> 00:31:56.580
We'll have a continuation of very high inflation.

00:31:56.580 --> 00:32:02.680
At the same time, that we will be pushed towards...what
is it, a banana?

00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:09.880
And that the pressure towards pushing the
economy towards a recession as a result of

00:32:09.880 --> 00:32:15.040
the tight monetary and fiscal policies will
be severe so that we may have the recession

00:32:15.040 --> 00:32:18.080
as well as inflation at the same time.

00:32:18.080 --> 00:32:22.750
John: Because our time is limited, come to
grip...I ask you to come to grips with some

00:32:22.750 --> 00:32:26.330
of the specifics of the program as it's been
announced.

00:32:26.330 --> 00:32:28.890
Let's go to real wage insurance, Dr. Kahn.

00:32:28.890 --> 00:32:35.070
Dr. Kahn: I find the real wage insurance plan
a very attractive and imaginative one.

00:32:35.070 --> 00:32:37.331
John: Would you outline it for the television
viewers?

00:32:37.331 --> 00:32:38.331
Dr. Kahn: Yes.

00:32:38.331 --> 00:32:46.460
The general notion is that workers who will
settle for wage increases within the 7% standard,

00:32:46.460 --> 00:32:52.850
in a sense making an act of faith that they
will not suffer in consequence from a failure

00:32:52.850 --> 00:32:58.510
of others to follow will be insulated against
an increase in the cost of living beyond 7%

00:32:58.510 --> 00:33:04.420
by having a tax rebate for the difference
between the 7% at which they settle and whatever

00:33:04.420 --> 00:33:06.240
the cost of living increase is.

00:33:06.240 --> 00:33:11.960
It's a variant on the tax incentive plans
which try to build in to the tax system a

00:33:11.960 --> 00:33:18.660
certain incentive to behave in a socially
responsible way in an area which is not subject

00:33:18.660 --> 00:33:19.660
to perfect competition.

00:33:19.660 --> 00:33:27.370
Man: That presupposes that the reporting that
will be made by the extraordinary numbers

00:33:27.370 --> 00:33:30.190
of millions of reports will be accurate.

00:33:30.190 --> 00:33:35.721
I think we know in advance the way the system
works, that it will be biased and biased in

00:33:35.721 --> 00:33:41.120
a very specific way so that it's quite conceivable
to me that you can have the Bureau of Labor

00:33:41.120 --> 00:33:48.130
Statistics report an 8% or 9% wage increase
and have everybody reporting that they complied

00:33:48.130 --> 00:33:49.350
under 7%.

00:33:49.350 --> 00:33:51.380
That is not something which I find utterly
shocking.

00:33:51.380 --> 00:33:53.940
Dr. Kahn: I can't really assess that.

00:33:53.940 --> 00:33:55.350
Perhaps you have some basis.

00:33:55.350 --> 00:34:00.530
Man: No, our experience with that type of
reporting when there is a reason to report

00:34:00.530 --> 00:34:07.240
in certain manner, very significantly balances
the numbers.

00:34:07.240 --> 00:34:08.240
Man: All right.

00:34:08.240 --> 00:34:12.560
I was gonna say, first of all, that it's superficially
looks like a very attractive plan.

00:34:12.560 --> 00:34:15.120
It has a very nice ring to it.

00:34:15.120 --> 00:34:19.690
I think the more you look at it in detail,
the worst it looks at least so it seems to

00:34:19.690 --> 00:34:20.950
me.

00:34:20.950 --> 00:34:25.999
One reason for that is that you have a wide
variety of sizes, the wage increases that

00:34:25.999 --> 00:34:27.589
occur every year.

00:34:27.589 --> 00:34:35.999
Take, for example, in 1977, first year wage
increases of major unions averaged about 8%,

00:34:35.999 --> 00:34:42.951
but about 30% of the people getting increases
under those agreements got increases of less

00:34:42.951 --> 00:34:44.509
than 7%.

00:34:44.509 --> 00:34:51.609
About 20% got increases of more than 9% so
that about 30% under such a scheme might well

00:34:51.609 --> 00:34:53.319
get some real wage insurance.

00:34:53.319 --> 00:34:59.319
For a large number of other people who got
wage increases, they wouldn't really have

00:34:59.319 --> 00:35:04.079
any interest in this program that just allows
them to keep up with inflation when they could

00:35:04.079 --> 00:35:06.940
probably do better in their own bargaining.

00:35:06.940 --> 00:35:13.790
So I think that it has a very haphazard kind
of distributional consequence in that regard.

00:35:13.790 --> 00:35:18.420
What also makes it I think extremely difficult
is the kind of information that you would

00:35:18.420 --> 00:35:23.060
need to have reported if you are going to
pursue such a scheme.

00:35:23.060 --> 00:35:29.619
And all the complexities would have to be
spelled out in sufficient detail so that every

00:35:29.619 --> 00:35:35.060
IRS auditor would be able to take a look at
the situation like they do individual income

00:35:35.060 --> 00:35:36.450
tax returns now.

00:35:36.450 --> 00:35:41.760
Man: Well, I will be only brief because I
will shortly exhaust my mind of information

00:35:41.760 --> 00:35:44.260
on the subject.

00:35:44.260 --> 00:35:49.789
The Treasury experts assure us that there's
no significant difficulty in that kind of

00:35:49.789 --> 00:35:51.480
reporting.

00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:56.559
On new contracts, it would be necessary to
have some sort of a certification by the commission

00:35:56.559 --> 00:35:57.559
on Wage and Price Stability.

00:35:57.559 --> 00:36:02.190
But I had understood that you, for years,
were in the business of certifying whether

00:36:02.190 --> 00:36:07.200
a settlement did or did not fall within certain
guidelines.

00:36:07.200 --> 00:36:08.279
I'm not making a debating point.

00:36:08.279 --> 00:36:09.279
I'm may be exaggerating.

00:36:09.279 --> 00:36:10.869
Man: That's why I'm very surprised that [crosstalk
00:36:11]

00:36:10.869 --> 00:36:12.769
Man: ...consequence of what's happening now.

00:36:12.769 --> 00:36:14.079
Yeah, I just wanna make that last point.

00:36:14.079 --> 00:36:16.349
Man: I think the Treasury is over-simplifying.

00:36:16.349 --> 00:36:23.420
What they're basically saying is that you
take an average earning's figure.

00:36:23.420 --> 00:36:29.089
The difficulty is that you have a mix problem,
difficulties of knowing whether you're looking

00:36:29.089 --> 00:36:32.569
at a promotion or whether you're looking at
a wage increase.

00:36:32.569 --> 00:36:34.550
You have a problem of part time workers.

00:36:34.550 --> 00:36:36.770
You have a problem of bonus workers.

00:36:36.770 --> 00:36:40.329
I may be the only private employer on this
panel.

00:36:40.329 --> 00:36:45.940
And I would be very hesitant to sign that
W-2 form because I'm not terribly certain

00:36:45.940 --> 00:36:51.430
whether the raises I get are in fact a wage
increase, a promotion, or what.

00:36:51.430 --> 00:36:56.490
And that's true of most or a very substantial
part of small business and a very substantial

00:36:56.490 --> 00:36:59.210
part of non-union labor.

00:36:59.210 --> 00:37:04.880
I think it's a tremendously complex program,
which we're only seeing the tip of an iceberg.

00:37:04.880 --> 00:37:10.079
I suspect the complexities of this issue are
going to baffle us.

00:37:10.079 --> 00:37:14.200
John: I think, for the layman, there is one
question which all four of you could help

00:37:14.200 --> 00:37:15.480
us with.

00:37:15.480 --> 00:37:20.289
We are not necessarily very understanding
of highly technical terms, but we have certain...in

00:37:20.289 --> 00:37:27.670
recent times, particularly heard so much about
monetary policy as a principal and most effective

00:37:27.670 --> 00:37:30.680
weapon against inflation.

00:37:30.680 --> 00:37:35.859
Is there agreement that it should have greater
weight than other programs or is it just equal

00:37:35.859 --> 00:37:37.829
to fiscal policy?

00:37:37.829 --> 00:37:39.450
Dr. Greenspan.

00:37:39.450 --> 00:37:47.140
Dr. Greenspan: I think what we know is that
when you have excessive monetary growth, you

00:37:47.140 --> 00:37:51.549
invariably have inflation and vice versa.

00:37:51.549 --> 00:37:56.309
And so that I would say, yes, money supply
is very critical.

00:37:56.309 --> 00:38:03.150
But I would not merely focus on it as something
you can turn on and off because it, too, is

00:38:03.150 --> 00:38:05.400
very critically determined by other things.

00:38:05.400 --> 00:38:11.970
In my view, before you can get a grip on money
supply growth and hence on inflation, you've

00:38:11.970 --> 00:38:17.509
got to reduce the aggregate credit requirements
in the economy, meaning you've got to reduce

00:38:17.509 --> 00:38:22.309
the federal borrowing requirements, which
comes from the budget deficit, from off budget

00:38:22.309 --> 00:38:23.799
financing.

00:38:23.799 --> 00:38:30.549
You've got to reduce the credit requirements
which the federal government indirectly requires

00:38:30.549 --> 00:38:38.019
of the private sector by mandating certain
regulations on safety and environment which

00:38:38.019 --> 00:38:41.099
they must borrow against to meet.

00:38:41.099 --> 00:38:45.320
I think the federal government in many of
its grants and aid program induces state and

00:38:45.320 --> 00:38:47.009
local governments to borrow.

00:38:47.009 --> 00:38:53.099
Finally, I think the most important thing
at this particular stage is the tremendous

00:38:53.099 --> 00:38:57.789
expansion in the mortgage market, which has
occurred in large part as a consequence of

00:38:57.789 --> 00:39:02.049
government subsidy direct and indirect.

00:39:02.049 --> 00:39:06.730
And that is what's creating a money supply
problem at this particular point.

00:39:06.730 --> 00:39:13.119
And unless we can come to grips with that
particular problem, I do not envisage the

00:39:13.119 --> 00:39:19.009
Federal Reserve being capable of restraining
the growth of money supply to non-inflationary

00:39:19.009 --> 00:39:22.640
dimensions without creating an explosive increase
in interest rates.

00:39:22.640 --> 00:39:23.640
John: Dr. Oswald.

00:39:23.640 --> 00:39:27.309
Dr. Oswald: I think I just wanted to amplify
a little bit what you've said, Alan, because

00:39:27.309 --> 00:39:31.450
I think some of the money is now being used
for corporate mergers.

00:39:31.450 --> 00:39:37.900
It's being used for the expansion of maybe
some of the new gambling activities in New

00:39:37.900 --> 00:39:40.010
Jersey and other places.

00:39:40.010 --> 00:39:47.869
Things up in that sense are not the new production
that leads to things and it may be more of

00:39:47.869 --> 00:39:52.420
shifting around of money than demand for goods
and services.

00:39:52.420 --> 00:39:59.970
And I'm not sure that the traditional Federal
Reserve Board actions get at some of the credit

00:39:59.970 --> 00:40:06.660
creation that is taking place through new
means of creating credit through the stock

00:40:06.660 --> 00:40:14.550
markets, options markets, through futures
markets, through all sorts of new means of

00:40:14.550 --> 00:40:15.640
creating credit.

00:40:15.640 --> 00:40:22.089
And I'm not sure that we're really addressing
that outside of just raising interest rates

00:40:22.089 --> 00:40:25.910
that I think is fairly harmful for a number
of people within the economy.

00:40:25.910 --> 00:40:29.749
John: I would like to suggest, gentlemen,
we have put down a very broad base on the

00:40:29.749 --> 00:40:34.809
administration's program and the entire subject
and it's time for us to move to the question

00:40:34.809 --> 00:40:36.339
and answer session.

00:40:36.339 --> 00:40:40.720
In the past 30 years, this country has gone
through six recessions.

00:40:40.720 --> 00:40:44.920
What steps is Congress likely to take to prevent
another one?

00:40:44.920 --> 00:40:47.390
Will federal budget cuts do the trick?

00:40:47.390 --> 00:40:50.309
At what point will we risk a recession?

00:40:50.309 --> 00:40:54.570
Aside from mandatory wage and price controls,
what should Congress and the President be

00:40:54.570 --> 00:40:56.920
planning for such an emergency?

00:40:56.920 --> 00:41:01.989
Now, to challenge our panel members, let's
get the views of the experts in our audience.

00:41:01.989 --> 00:41:02.989
All right.

00:41:02.989 --> 00:41:04.849
May I have the first question please?

00:41:04.849 --> 00:41:07.390
Larry: Larry Rosenberg, National Science Foundation.

00:41:07.390 --> 00:41:15.109
The question is addressed to the panel at
large but perhaps first to Professor Kahn.

00:41:15.109 --> 00:41:22.180
Many of the suggested solutions to inflation
are of a longer term structural nature, particularly

00:41:22.180 --> 00:41:27.329
seemingly the suggestions to make markets
more efficient and more competitive.

00:41:27.329 --> 00:41:32.900
Within that realm, do you see any possibilities
for shorter range targets?

00:41:32.900 --> 00:41:38.720
Dr. Kahn: Well, the targets will be very short
range.

00:41:38.720 --> 00:41:44.519
I expect within the course of the year that
there will be definite movements in the direction

00:41:44.519 --> 00:41:50.499
of the deregulation of trucking, for example,
the more thorough deregulation of the railroads.

00:41:50.499 --> 00:41:54.720
There will be active discussions of the extent
to which we can have more competition in the

00:41:54.720 --> 00:41:59.009
maritime shipping field rather than move toward
tighter and tighter cartelization.

00:41:59.009 --> 00:42:04.819
The area of agricultural, restrictions on
output, now don't expect the Millennium.

00:42:04.819 --> 00:42:09.250
But certainly, one can move in the direction
always of the less restrictive approach toward

00:42:09.250 --> 00:42:12.099
maintaining farm income trade restrictions.

00:42:12.099 --> 00:42:16.440
After all, the president just vetoed that
cotton textiles protective legislation.

00:42:16.440 --> 00:42:21.240
He vetoed the beef quarter bill that would
have tied his hands more.

00:42:21.240 --> 00:42:23.940
Perhaps that gives you enough.

00:42:23.940 --> 00:42:26.880
I mean, we're interested in the state regulation
of insurance.

00:42:26.880 --> 00:42:30.849
I mean, do you know, for example, that in
the states in which you have open filing that

00:42:30.849 --> 00:42:35.950
is more possibility of price competition that
you have markedly lower insurance rates for

00:42:35.950 --> 00:42:40.619
liability and property insurance than you
have in another states?

00:42:40.619 --> 00:42:44.309
It's kind of a general attitude toward every
way in which the government subsidizes and

00:42:44.309 --> 00:42:45.470
protects and limits competition.

00:42:45.470 --> 00:42:47.810
And I expect it to produce some results.

00:42:47.810 --> 00:42:48.810
John: Yes, doctor.

00:42:48.810 --> 00:42:54.849
Man: I don't see that this is really a change
from what government has tried to do always

00:42:54.849 --> 00:43:01.920
in terms of trying to affect the economy and
in terms of results, so I don't anticipate

00:43:01.920 --> 00:43:06.440
that there will be results in the next 12
months from these types of programs.

00:43:06.440 --> 00:43:12.260
Man: Well, look, I don't promise miracles,
but we had an airline regulation system for

00:43:12.260 --> 00:43:15.720
40 years, and we changed it this year.

00:43:15.720 --> 00:43:18.779
Maybe people will learn that it's not so horrible
after all.

00:43:18.779 --> 00:43:20.220
We can make more money.

00:43:20.220 --> 00:43:21.640
We can bring bargains to travelers.

00:43:21.640 --> 00:43:25.470
We can even have a 3% reduction in price at
the time when the CPI went up 7%.

00:43:25.470 --> 00:43:27.710
Man: I think that's a terribly important example.

00:43:27.710 --> 00:43:28.710
John: All right.

00:43:28.710 --> 00:43:29.710
Next question please.

00:43:29.710 --> 00:43:30.710
Yes, Sir.

00:43:30.710 --> 00:43:33.430
John: I'm John Kendrick of George Washington
University.

00:43:33.430 --> 00:43:36.900
And my question is primarily for Rudy Oswald.

00:43:36.900 --> 00:43:44.700
In view of the preponderance of labor costs
and price, do you think it's possible to wind

00:43:44.700 --> 00:43:52.509
down inflation without reducing the rate of
increase in wage rates or average hourly compensation?

00:43:52.509 --> 00:43:56.750
And if you agree with that, let me ask you,
how would you recommend that we gradually

00:43:56.750 --> 00:44:01.119
decelerate the rate of increase in wage rates
in the economy?

00:44:01.119 --> 00:44:10.380
Dr. Oswald: Kendrick, I think that you've
written many books on the impact of productivity

00:44:10.380 --> 00:44:18.499
in the economy and the ability to have real
gains in the economy as a result of productivity.

00:44:18.499 --> 00:44:26.009
Well, part of the gains of productivity can
be used to slow down productivity.

00:44:26.009 --> 00:44:34.630
When I spoke before in terms of Dr. Kahn's
notion of looking at sectorial problems, I

00:44:34.630 --> 00:44:42.720
think that there are certain sectorial problems
that can be addressed that are not directly

00:44:42.720 --> 00:44:44.789
wage related.

00:44:44.789 --> 00:44:53.940
And in general, wage changes in the last year,
last three years have not been particularly

00:44:53.940 --> 00:44:56.369
pressing in terms of pushing prices up.

00:44:56.369 --> 00:45:00.319
There have been substantial attempts to catch
up.

00:45:00.319 --> 00:45:07.660
But that's an attempt to say that when somebody
works, he expects to be able to have the ability

00:45:07.660 --> 00:45:11.650
to purchase something with what he works for
rather than just paper.

00:45:11.650 --> 00:45:14.160
Man: Can I just say one word about that?

00:45:14.160 --> 00:45:19.089
I have no intention of putting...ascribing
blame particularly to one part of the economy

00:45:19.089 --> 00:45:20.089
or another.

00:45:20.089 --> 00:45:24.619
But you really have missed the basic arithmetic
point that Dr. Kendrick made that if 70% of

00:45:24.619 --> 00:45:28.999
your sales prices or of your gross national
product goes to labor, then there's absolutely

00:45:28.999 --> 00:45:34.579
no way in the world in which you can have
wage increases of 10%, 11%, 9%, or even 7%

00:45:34.579 --> 00:45:39.940
a year when we have never come remotely close
on any long range basis to producing productivity

00:45:39.940 --> 00:45:45.130
increases of more than 3 and now we're nearly
at the 2% level without getting something

00:45:45.130 --> 00:45:49.630
like giving those different examples, 6%,
7%, 8% of inflation per year.

00:45:49.630 --> 00:45:51.170
And that's the simple answer to his question.

00:45:51.170 --> 00:45:53.220
There is no way.

00:45:53.220 --> 00:45:56.140
And I guess that's a matter of simple arithmetic.

00:45:56.140 --> 00:45:59.099
The other point I wanted to make was catch
up.

00:45:59.099 --> 00:46:03.700
You will always find at least 50% of the population
who have not done as well in the preceding

00:46:03.700 --> 00:46:04.890
years the other 50%.

00:46:04.890 --> 00:46:08.559
Actually, if you know, the way these frequency
distributions are spread, it's more than 50%

00:46:08.559 --> 00:46:10.430
that do less than average.

00:46:10.430 --> 00:46:15.940
If you always, always say anybody who is falling
behind has a right to catch up, then you have

00:46:15.940 --> 00:46:18.349
built in a permanent engine of inflation.

00:46:18.349 --> 00:46:19.730
Catch-up equals inflation.

00:46:19.730 --> 00:46:21.349
John: Dr. Kosters.

00:46:21.349 --> 00:46:27.069
Dr. Kosters: I certainly agree with the point
on arithmetic that is there is no way in which

00:46:27.069 --> 00:46:32.000
you can get inflation to slow down without
having commensurate slowdown in the rate of

00:46:32.000 --> 00:46:33.089
wage increase.

00:46:33.089 --> 00:46:36.609
But as I understand this program, something
more is intended.

00:46:36.609 --> 00:46:45.109
And that is, if I understood kind of correctly
earlier, what he's saying is that the program

00:46:45.109 --> 00:46:50.380
plans to look at situations where competition
in the labor market is less prevalent.

00:46:50.380 --> 00:46:56.779
Now, it seems to me that that makes sense
if you can induce the institutional change

00:46:56.779 --> 00:47:02.630
that makes the situation more competitive
but to use wage controls or a wage standard

00:47:02.630 --> 00:47:08.029
or some kind of wage price policy to get a
result that you would not get with the conditions

00:47:08.029 --> 00:47:13.130
of competition in that industry, in that sector,
I think is likely to fail.

00:47:13.130 --> 00:47:16.749
Man: I think this is so important that I...forgive
me for another second.

00:47:16.749 --> 00:47:17.749
John: Please do.

00:47:17.749 --> 00:47:22.059
Man: We're asking where are the signs of excessive
aggregate demand, too much spending in the

00:47:22.059 --> 00:47:24.690
economy that is pushing wages up?

00:47:24.690 --> 00:47:27.230
And we see very little signs of that in most
parts of the economy.

00:47:27.230 --> 00:47:31.390
You may say there's excessive spending for
housing and yet construction wages have risen

00:47:31.390 --> 00:47:33.540
much less than the average.

00:47:33.540 --> 00:47:38.029
And my answer is that the basic problem of
inflation is a social problem.

00:47:38.029 --> 00:47:44.160
It is a mark of a society in which people
are constantly increasing their income claims,

00:47:44.160 --> 00:47:48.960
both in the wages they demand, in the profit
margin that they add to their wage cost, in

00:47:48.960 --> 00:47:53.030
the demands that they place on government
for tax preferences or subsidies.

00:47:53.030 --> 00:47:57.420
In the demands for government spending programs,
they all add up to more than we can supply

00:47:57.420 --> 00:47:59.030
at constant prices.

00:47:59.030 --> 00:48:00.859
And that's what we've been witnessing.

00:48:00.859 --> 00:48:03.020
It's justified by some on the grounds of catch
up.

00:48:03.020 --> 00:48:05.940
It's justified by others on the ground that
they're in industries which are above average

00:48:05.940 --> 00:48:07.210
and productivity.

00:48:07.210 --> 00:48:12.079
What we see than is a problem that seems to
us is not easily solvable simply by putting

00:48:12.079 --> 00:48:15.789
mark aggregate restraints because that will
cause great unemployment.

00:48:15.789 --> 00:48:19.029
It's simple enough to throw 20 million people
out of work, and then they will moderate their

00:48:19.029 --> 00:48:20.329
wage demands.

00:48:20.329 --> 00:48:25.119
We have to do something by way of inducing
a general spirit of social discipline which

00:48:25.119 --> 00:48:30.979
alone can come in my judgment only by a recognition
that we're all like hamsters in a cage running

00:48:30.979 --> 00:48:33.990
as fast as we can and staying in the same
place.

00:48:33.990 --> 00:48:38.609
I think there's a general recognition that
we've all gotta moderate in some way.

00:48:38.609 --> 00:48:41.609
And I don't know how to do that by simply
imposing monetary and fiscal restraints without

00:48:41.609 --> 00:48:42.609
serious unemployment.

00:48:42.609 --> 00:48:47.630
Dr. Kosters: Let me expand to that very briefly.

00:48:47.630 --> 00:48:48.770
Dr. Kosters.

00:48:48.770 --> 00:48:55.539
It seems to me that our problem so far this
year has not been a matter of wages rising

00:48:55.539 --> 00:48:59.319
faster than prices or in any sense pushing
prices up.

00:48:59.319 --> 00:49:05.819
It seems to me clearly to not have come from
the side of workers all saying to themselves,

00:49:05.819 --> 00:49:07.150
"We can't fall behind.

00:49:07.150 --> 00:49:09.549
We got to catch up or we got to do better."

00:49:09.549 --> 00:49:11.981
Instead, it seems to have come on the price
side.

00:49:11.981 --> 00:49:17.960
That is to say prices have risen a good deal
more during the course of the year than have

00:49:17.960 --> 00:49:18.960
wages.

00:49:18.960 --> 00:49:24.130
It seems to me very difficult to argue that
that our problem now is those costs pushing

00:49:24.130 --> 00:49:29.690
themselves up autonomously rather than that
it's demand pushing prices up.

00:49:29.690 --> 00:49:30.690
John: Dr. Greenspan.

00:49:30.690 --> 00:49:37.589
Dr. Greenspan: The problem that we have got
is one which we have no firm theoretical understanding

00:49:37.589 --> 00:49:39.119
of.

00:49:39.119 --> 00:49:44.410
We have for the last six or seven years been
endeavoring to understand the theoretical

00:49:44.410 --> 00:49:50.789
framework which constructs a situation in
which we have chronic inflammation in a period

00:49:50.789 --> 00:49:54.410
of less than full utilization of resources.

00:49:54.410 --> 00:50:00.369
It is not an explanation to say that there
are excess demands in the social system, because

00:50:00.369 --> 00:50:06.380
that translated into the mechanisms of an
economy is a demand pull situation.

00:50:06.380 --> 00:50:10.970
The problem that we have got is not something
we fully understand.

00:50:10.970 --> 00:50:16.920
When we endeavor to create the relationship
of wages and prices in our econometric models,

00:50:16.920 --> 00:50:20.499
we have found them wholly inadequate.

00:50:20.499 --> 00:50:22.529
We have been groping for answers.

00:50:22.529 --> 00:50:27.829
And I think at this stage, the results to
date have been most unsatisfactory.

00:50:27.829 --> 00:50:35.049
I would not conclude from that that therefore,
we then presume that because we see a relationship

00:50:35.049 --> 00:50:42.349
between wages and prices, which does exist,
that we can somehow create the symptoms of

00:50:42.349 --> 00:50:44.680
the problem and presume the issue will be
resolved.

00:50:44.680 --> 00:50:50.230
I think the experience that we have had in
endeavoring to bring down inflation by bringing

00:50:50.230 --> 00:50:58.710
down wages or bringing down wages by bringing
down prices invariably has failed and fallen

00:50:58.710 --> 00:51:00.119
apart.

00:51:00.119 --> 00:51:05.430
All that we can observe is that these particular
efforts at creating the symptoms have not

00:51:05.430 --> 00:51:07.150
worked.

00:51:07.150 --> 00:51:18.059
And I think that to call for a particular
form of restraint, which somehow simultaneously

00:51:18.059 --> 00:51:23.190
will bring the whole system down, I think
flies in the face of the experiences that

00:51:23.190 --> 00:51:29.069
we and other countries have had in this whole
particular area.

00:51:29.069 --> 00:51:35.210
There has been one of the explanations which
is this issue of inflationary expectations

00:51:35.210 --> 00:51:39.180
as though that explains the phenomenon.

00:51:39.180 --> 00:51:48.390
Well, it doesn't because the mere fact of
people expecting prices to rise further will

00:51:48.390 --> 00:51:53.019
certainly induce them to take certain actions,
but it's not clear which direction.

00:51:53.019 --> 00:51:59.039
In fact, our experience when people are concerned
about inflation is that they don't spend more.

00:51:59.039 --> 00:52:00.180
They save more.

00:52:00.180 --> 00:52:05.029
They can track their actions which is actually
an anti-inflationary force.

00:52:05.029 --> 00:52:06.809
So that doesn't explain it.

00:52:06.809 --> 00:52:09.890
I think what we have is a tremendously serious
problem.

00:52:09.890 --> 00:52:16.619
I am very much concerned that we all grant
that there's some fundamental solutions to

00:52:16.619 --> 00:52:20.819
this and we keep saying that we can't.

00:52:20.819 --> 00:52:22.790
We have to create 20 million unemployed.

00:52:22.790 --> 00:52:27.559
But that I find rather rhetorical to say the
least.

00:52:27.559 --> 00:52:32.400
What concerns me is that we know what the
problem is.

00:52:32.400 --> 00:52:38.010
We say it is politically, socially, and otherwise
unacceptable to do this, that, or the other

00:52:38.010 --> 00:52:39.190
thing.

00:52:39.190 --> 00:52:43.670
And we find that as we procrastinate, the
problem gets worse.

00:52:43.670 --> 00:52:49.710
And the form of the socially and politically
unacceptable alternative gets worse every

00:52:49.710 --> 00:52:50.710
year.

00:52:50.710 --> 00:52:55.900
I submit at some point, we're gonna have to
come to grips with this problem and work very

00:52:55.900 --> 00:53:02.489
hard to resolve it and not continuously look
for solutions for which there are no costs.

00:53:02.489 --> 00:53:05.690
John: May I have the next question.

00:53:05.690 --> 00:53:06.690
Sir.

00:53:06.690 --> 00:53:07.690
Dan: Yes.

00:53:07.690 --> 00:53:09.339
Dan [inaudible 00:53:07], Fairchild Publications.

00:53:09.339 --> 00:53:14.080
When the President's program was first announced,
he indicated that the federal government would

00:53:14.080 --> 00:53:21.119
use its purchasing power to encourage compliance
by not buying from contractors who violated

00:53:21.119 --> 00:53:22.119
the guidelines.

00:53:22.119 --> 00:53:28.569
My question is, are you moving toward asking
the nation's consumers to boycott companies

00:53:28.569 --> 00:53:31.859
that violate the price guidelines?

00:53:31.859 --> 00:53:39.549
Man: We haven't done so explicitly.

00:53:39.549 --> 00:53:45.710
And I'm sorry to say that I have not yet reached
the point of being able to give you an adequate

00:53:45.710 --> 00:53:47.500
answer.

00:53:47.500 --> 00:53:53.430
You must recognize, however, that what we
have is a program that is in very large measure

00:53:53.430 --> 00:54:00.869
voluntary and is indeed being enforced by
something on the order of 100 people.

00:54:00.869 --> 00:54:07.109
And we are determined to fashion a program
which will not require any more than 100 people.

00:54:07.109 --> 00:54:09.680
There's a tremendous amount of self-enforcement
here.

00:54:09.680 --> 00:54:14.529
Now, in those circumstances where you're depending
very largely on volition, and you're not going

00:54:14.529 --> 00:54:17.849
to have a field of inspectors who are going
to be able to look at prices and individual

00:54:17.849 --> 00:54:25.109
grocery stores or the rents charged or the
prices charged for haircuts, I think that

00:54:25.109 --> 00:54:32.539
one very important enforcement mechanism will
have to be a kind of sensitization of consumers

00:54:32.539 --> 00:54:39.339
to this inflation and decisions by consumers
not to buy from people who are raising their

00:54:39.339 --> 00:54:40.339
prices excessively.

00:54:40.339 --> 00:54:41.339
John: Dr. Oswald.

00:54:41.339 --> 00:54:42.339
Dr. Oswald.

00:54:42.339 --> 00:54:46.890
Well, I think that's expecting something that
the guidelines don't even allow somebody to

00:54:46.890 --> 00:54:47.890
know.

00:54:47.890 --> 00:54:53.900
For example, on November 16th, Newsweek Magazine
raised the price of the magazine from a dollar

00:54:53.900 --> 00:55:00.380
to a dollar and a quarter but there's no way
of an individual knowing whether that's in

00:55:00.380 --> 00:55:04.500
compliance with the guidelines or not.

00:55:04.500 --> 00:55:12.269
And yet that's a 25% increase in terms of
a product that is sold on the newsstands.

00:55:12.269 --> 00:55:17.369
I think the expectation that has been given
by the President's message is that there is

00:55:17.369 --> 00:55:22.410
a government who is concerned, has set guidelines
and will do something.

00:55:22.410 --> 00:55:28.559
And yet you tell them on the wage side, employers
will do something, but on the price side,

00:55:28.559 --> 00:55:29.559
nothing will be done.

00:55:29.559 --> 00:55:31.299
Well, I think that's absurd.

00:55:31.299 --> 00:55:36.810
I mean, we don't have any authority, any more
authority to do something about wages in a

00:55:36.810 --> 00:55:42.579
particular locality than we have to do about
prices in that locality.

00:55:42.579 --> 00:55:47.289
So if you're gonna talk about the limitations
of our enforcement mechanisms, those limitations

00:55:47.289 --> 00:55:49.450
apply both to the wage and the price side.

00:55:49.450 --> 00:55:56.089
Man: Except that on the wage side, you do
have an organized mechanism of enforcers,

00:55:56.089 --> 00:55:57.569
employers.

00:55:57.569 --> 00:55:59.990
There is no organized group of consumers.

00:55:59.990 --> 00:56:02.769
Man: Oh, that's not true.

00:56:02.769 --> 00:56:09.469
Man: I think that the presumption that employers
somehow would like to keep the wages down

00:56:09.469 --> 00:56:16.119
no matter what, I think misses how wages are
set in a non-union area.

00:56:16.119 --> 00:56:21.319
Wages are set not by the benevolence of the
employer but by their need to keep their good

00:56:21.319 --> 00:56:26.780
workers in place, producing the goods that
they have to sell to their customers.

00:56:26.780 --> 00:56:35.119
If they arbitrarily endeavored to restrain
wages below some particular market level,

00:56:35.119 --> 00:56:41.390
they would find a substantial part of their
most productive workforce going to their competitors.

00:56:41.390 --> 00:56:52.160
And I would think that that view that somehow
that the employers are controlling the wages

00:56:52.160 --> 00:56:56.869
and are, in fact, the wage enforcers I think
badly miss reads how the system functions.

00:56:56.869 --> 00:56:57.869
John: Dr. Kosters.

00:56:57.869 --> 00:57:03.579
Dr. Kosters: I'd like to point out an oddity
about the way this kind of enforcement mechanism

00:57:03.579 --> 00:57:04.579
works.

00:57:04.579 --> 00:57:09.809
You have a situation where the government
is getting procurement bids and so on.

00:57:09.809 --> 00:57:16.479
And as I understand it, instead of taking
the low bid if that firm was out of compliance

00:57:16.479 --> 00:57:22.739
because it granted a large wage increase,
it would take the higher bid.

00:57:22.739 --> 00:57:28.859
And it seems to me that what we don't want
to encourage is firm behavior that has high

00:57:28.859 --> 00:57:31.910
prices and doesn't raise wages very much.

00:57:31.910 --> 00:57:37.900
And it seems to me to be a mistake to encourage
consumers to patronize that kind of firm as

00:57:37.900 --> 00:57:38.900
well.

00:57:38.900 --> 00:57:39.900
John: All right.

00:57:39.900 --> 00:57:43.970
This concludes another Public Policy Forum
presented by the American Enterprise Institute

00:57:43.970 --> 00:57:46.029
for Public Policy Research.

00:57:46.029 --> 00:57:50.819
This Public Policy Forum on Weapons Against
Inflation has brought you the views of four

00:57:50.819 --> 00:57:52.700
experts in the field.

00:57:52.700 --> 00:57:57.329
It was presented by AEI, the American Enterprise
Institute.

00:57:57.329 --> 00:58:03.420
It is the aim of AEI to clarify issues of
the day by presenting many viewpoints in the

00:58:03.420 --> 00:58:09.819
hope that by so doing, those who wish to learn
about the decision making process will benefit

00:58:09.819 --> 00:58:13.900
from such a free exchange of informed and
enlightened opinion.

